# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
plato-phaedo-1.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 3 ways: plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1 has "fearless ∧ noble_bearing" and "fearless ∧ noble_bearing → appeared_blessed" and "hardly_believe → ¬pity"; plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1.2 has "fearless" and "noble_bearing" and "fearless ∧ noble_bearing → appeared_blessed" and "hardly_believe"; they differ when only "he died fearlessly", "his words and bearing were noble and gracious" and "to me he appeared blessed" are true, which the first allows; plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1.3 has "fearless ∧ noble_bearing" and "fearless ∧ noble_bearing → appeared_blessed" and "hardly_believe" and "hardly_believe ∧ appeared_blessed → ¬pity"
plato-phaedo-1.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1.2 and plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "he died fearlessly", "his words and bearing were noble and gracious" and "to me he appeared blessed" are true and "I could hardly believe that I was present at the death of a friend" and "I pitied him" are false. Is S2, "For I could hardly believe that I was present at the death of a friend, and therefore I did not pity him, Echecrates; he died so fearlessly, and his words and bearing were so noble and gracious, that to me he appeared blessed.", true then?
plato-phaedo-1.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1 and plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1.3 have "divine_call ∧ happy_there" and "divine_call ∧ happy_there → ¬pity"; plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1.2 has "divine_call" and "happy_there"; they differ when only "I pitied him", "in going to the other world he could not be without a divine call" and "he would be happy, if any man ever was, when he arrived there" are true, which the second allows
plato-phaedo-1.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1 and plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "I pitied him", "in going to the other world he could not be without a divine call" and "he would be happy, if any man ever was, when he arrived there" are true. Is S3, "I thought that in going to the other world he could not be without a divine call, and that he would be happy, if any man ever was, when he arrived there, and therefore I did not pity him as might have seemed natural at such an hour.", true then?
plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1 has "appeared_blessed → hardly_believe"; plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1.2 has "hardly_believe → ¬pity" and "divine_call ∧ happy_there → ¬pity"; they differ when only "to me he appeared blessed", "I could hardly believe that I was present at the death of a friend" and "I pitied him" are true, which the first allows; plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1.3 has nothing from it
